```markdown
---
title: "General Purpose Search Engine"
category: "Social & Discovery"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["search", "indexing", "ranking", "crawling", "information-retrieval"]
---

## Overview

This system crawls a large document corpus, turns raw content into an inverted index, and serves queries with low latency and high relevance. The key insight is to treat indexing as a **deterministic, replayable log pipeline** that produces **immutable index segments** (Lucene-style), so freshness and correctness come from idempotence + segment lifecycle—not from fragile “in-place” mutations.

The design stays boring where it should: Kafka for the ingest log, object storage for raw fetch artifacts, and a proven text indexing library for segment creation. The elegance is in two places: (1) **document identity + dedup + lifecycle** so “the same thing” doesn’t explode the index, and (2) **two-stage ranking** that keeps latency flat while still allowing rich relevance.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive teams underestimate how quickly crawl + index becomes inconsistent: the same URL produces different bytes over time, redirects change canonical identity, boilerplate dominates tokens, and duplicates (near and exact) silently multiply storage and distort rank signals. “Just index what you fetch” yields an index that grows faster than content value.

On ranking, the trap is trying to do “smart” scoring in the first stage. If your first-stage retrieval isn’t brutally fast and recall-oriented, you either miss good results or blow your latency budget. Great search is usually **cheap recall first, expensive precision second**.

## Requirements

### Functional Requirements
- **Canonical document identity**: handle redirects, canonical tags, and content-based dedup so rank signals accumulate on the right entity.
- **Incremental updates**: re-crawls must update the index without full rebuilds; deletions and “gone” pages must decay out predictably.
- **Spam resistance (baseline)**: prevent obvious keyword stuffing, doorway pages, and duplicate farms from dominating.
- **Explainability hooks**: for any served result, produce a minimal “why” trace (top terms/features), or on-call cannot debug relevance.

### Scale Targets
- **Corpus**: 10B documents stored; 1B “active” (served) after dedup and quality filters.
- **Freshness**: 1% of corpus re-crawled daily (10M docs/day); hot set refreshed hourly.
- **Index size**: ~5–20% of raw text size (depends on fields, positions, and stored snippets).
- **Query load**: 50k QPS peak, p95 < 200ms end-to-end; top-200 candidate set reranked within ~30ms budget.

These numbers force: (1) sharded index with local SSD, (2) asynchronous indexing with replay, (3) two-stage retrieval.

## Key Design Decisions

- **Chose: immutable, segment-based inverted index**
  - Rejected: in-place per-document updates in a mutable global index
  - Why: segments make indexing replayable and safe; merges handle updates/deletes without coordination nightmares.

- **Chose: content-addressed storage + canonical ID**
  - Rejected: URL as the primary key
  - Why: URLs are not identities (redirects, tracking params, mirrors). Content hashes + canonicalization stabilize dedup and signal aggregation.

- **Chose: two-stage ranking (lexical retrieval → ML rerank)**
  - Rejected: “one big model” scoring every document
  - Why: retrieval must be cheap and high-recall; rerank spends compute only on a small candidate set.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Seed & Schedules] --> B[Crawler/Fetche​r]
  B --> C[Raw Store]
  B --> D[Fetch Log]
  D --> E[Parse & Tokenize]
  E --> F[Segment Builder]
  F --> G[Index Shards]
  H[Query API] --> G
  H --> I[Ranker]
```

### Components

- **Seed & Schedules**: owns crawl policy (freshness tiers, host politeness, per-host budgets). This earns its place because crawl order determines relevance and cost more than any micro-optimization later.
- **Crawler/Fetcher**: fetches with strong HTTP hygiene (redirect capture, content-type enforcement, robots compliance, timeouts). Emits artifacts and metadata, never “business logic.”
- **Raw Store (object storage)**: immutable storage of fetched bytes + extracted boilerplate-stripped text. This is the reprocessing escape hatch when tokenization or spam logic changes.
- **Fetch Log (Kafka)**: the source of truth for “what was fetched.” Replay turns production incidents into “rewind and rebuild,” not “restore from mystery state.”
- **Parse & Tokenize**: canonicalization, language detection, boilerplate removal, field extraction (title/body/anchors), and normalization. Output is a deterministic document representation.
- **Segment Builder**: batches documents into index segments with postings, positions, docvalues (for fast scoring), and stored snippets. Writes immutable segments and publishes a manifest.
- **Index Shards**: sharded, replicated set of segments on local SSD; periodic merges compact deletes and optimize term dictionaries.
- **Query API**: fan-out to shards, enforces time budgets, and returns candidates + traces.
- **Ranker**: stage-1 retrieval (BM25 + light boosts) then stage-2 rerank (LTR model) over top-K.

## Deep Dive: Document Identity, Updates, and Dedup (The Hardest Part)

The core problem: “a document” is not a URL, and indexing pipelines fail when identity shifts. We define three IDs:
1. **FetchID**: unique per fetch attempt (URL + timestamp + crawler config). This is purely operational.
2. **ContentID**: hash of normalized content bytes after canonicalization/boilerplate stripping (stable across mirrors and tracking params).
3. **DocID**: stable logical entity ID chosen by rules: canonical URL (if trustworthy) + ContentID tie-breakers, plus redirect chain resolution.

Pipeline behavior:
- The tokenizer emits `(DocID, ContentID, fields, signals, fetch_metadata)`.
- If the same DocID arrives with a new ContentID, it is an **update**. Updates are not “mutate postings”; instead we write a new segment containing the new version and mark the old version deleted via a **delete bitmap** keyed by DocID.
- For exact duplicates (same ContentID across many URLs), we create one “primary” DocID for indexing and store other URLs as aliases with lightweight metadata. This prevents link farms from multiplying postings and inflating popularity signals.

Near-duplicate handling is where teams bleed money. Use a cheap, deterministic fingerprint:
- Compute shingles (e.g., 5-grams) on normalized text, produce a SimHash/MinHash signature.
- Maintain a signature index per shard (small) to detect near-duplicates; if similarity > threshold, store as alias unless the page has meaningfully distinct fields (title/site/structured data).
This keeps the index aligned with *information content*, not with crawl volume.

Finally, lifecycle:
- “Gone” pages (404/410) are treated as tombstones that trigger deletes; tombstones expire after a policy window to handle transient outages.
- Segment merges physically drop deleted docs and stale aliases, preventing the index from becoming a graveyard.

## Trade-offs

| Optimized For | Sacrificed |
|--------------|------------|
| Replayable, correct indexing | Extra storage for raw artifacts |
| Low-latency queries at scale | Complexity in shard fan-out and time budgets |
| Stable identity and dedup | Some recall loss on aggressive near-dup thresholds |
| Fast iteration on ranking | Operational cost of feature pipelines/models |

## Failure Modes

- **Pipeline lag → stale results**
  - Detect: Kafka consumer lag, “freshness age” SLO per tier
  - Recover: autoscale tokenizers/builders, shed low-priority crawl tiers, replay from Fetch Log after fixes

- **Bad canonicalization/dedup → relevance collapse**
  - Detect: sudden drop in unique DocIDs indexed, spike in aliasing rate, A/B metrics regression
  - Recover: rollback tokenizer version, rebuild affected tiers from Raw Store, quarantine domains with outlier duplication

- **Shard degradation (slow or corrupt segments) → tail latency**
  - Detect: per-shard p95/p99, checksum failures, merge backlog
  - Recover: promote replica, rehydrate shard from segment manifests, throttle merges during peak

## What I'd Do Differently At...

- **10x scale:** move merges and segment distribution to a dedicated coordinator; introduce tiered storage (hot SSD, warm disk) and stricter crawl budgeting.
- **100x scale:** separate “retrieval index” from “feature store” entirely; adopt aggressive multi-tier retrieval (static top sites cache, query-result cache, learned sparse retrieval) and dedicated model inference fleet.

## Operational Notes

- Treat the **Fetch Log as sacred**: every production incident should be recoverable via replay + deterministic transforms.
- Maintain explicit **time budgets**: shard fan-out must degrade gracefully (return best-effort top-K rather than timing out the whole query).
- Watch **merge debt**: if merges fall behind, deletes accumulate and tail latency creeps up invisibly.
- Keep tokenizer changes **versioned and reversible**: canonicalization tweaks can silently rewrite identity; require shadow runs before full rollout.
