title: "General Purpose Search Engine"
category: "Social & Discovery"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["search", "indexing", "ranking", "crawling", "information-retrieval"]
---

## Overview

This system crawls a large document corpus, turns raw content into an inverted index, and serves queries with low latency and high relevance. The key insight is to treat indexing as a **deterministic, replayable log pipeline** that produces **immutable index segments** (Lucene-style), so freshness and correctness come from idempotence + segment lifecycle—not from fragile “in-place” mutations.

The design stays boring where it should: Kafka for the ingest log, object storage for raw fetch artifacts, and a proven text indexing library for segment creation. The elegance is in two places: (1) **document identity + dedup + lifecycle** so “the same thing” doesn’t explode the index, and (2) **two-stage ranking** that keeps latency flat while still allowing rich relevance.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive teams underestimate how quickly crawl + index becomes inconsistent: the same URL produces different bytes over time, redirects change canonical identity, boilerplate dominates tokens, and duplicates (near and exact) silently multiply storage and distort rank signals. “Just index what you fetch” yields an index that grows faster than content value.

On ranking, the trap is trying to do “smart” scoring in the first stage. If your first-stage retrieval isn’t brutally fast and recall-oriented, you either miss good results or blow your latency budget. Great search is usually **cheap recall first, expensive precision second**.

